foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F10.pic1.7" class="ltx_text"># Classes</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(0.0 1.0 -1.0 0.0 -11.82 127.94)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="83.06"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F10.pic1.8" class="ltx_text">Accuracy (%)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#000000"><path d="M 33.66 79.99 h 170.25 v 74.57 h -170.25 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 37.81 91.75)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 69.03)"><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 8.26)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="1.5pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.04,0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 11.81 0 L 23.62 0"></path><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#9999FF;" fill="#9999FF"><path d="M 11.81 2.77 L 14.21 -1.38 L 9.41 -1.38 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 64.64 0) translate(29.18,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -26.42 -3.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.82em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="52.83"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="A5.F10.pic1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o=1600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1600</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o=1600</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 24.79)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="1.5pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.04,0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 11.81 0 L 23.62 0"></path><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#0000FF;" fill="#0000FF"><path d="M 14.58 0 C 14.58 1.53 13.34 2.77 11.81 2.77 C 10.28 2.77 9.04 1.53 9.04 0 C 9.04 -1.53 10.28 -2.77 11.81 -2.77 C 13.34 -2.77 14.58 -1.53 14.58 0 Z M 11.81 0"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 64.64 0) translate(29.18,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -26.42 -3.42)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:3.82em;--ltx-fo-height:0.64em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="8.92" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 8.92)" width="52.83"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="A5.F10.pic1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o=6400" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6400</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o=6400</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 42.05)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="1.5pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.04,0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 11.81 0 L 23.62 0"></path><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FF9999;" fill="#FF9999"><path d="M 11.81 2.77 L 14.21 -1.38 L 9.41 -1.38 Z"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 29.16 0) translate(64.67,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -61.9 -4.15)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.18em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="85.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F10.pic1.9" class="ltx_text">Group-Sum (<math id="A5.F10.pic1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=30" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=30</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g><g class="ltx_tikzmatrix_row" transform="matrix(1 0 0 1 0 60.03)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-width="1.5pt" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(1.04,0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M 0 0 L 11.81 0 L 23.62 0"></path><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FF0000;" fill="#FF0000"><path d="M 14.58 0 C 14.58 1.53 13.34 2.77 11.81 2.77 C 10.28 2.77 9.04 1.53 9.04 0 C 9.04 -1.53 10.28 -2.77 11.81 -2.77 C 13.34 -2.77 14.58 -1.53 14.58 0 Z M 11.81 0"></path></g></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" class="ltx_tikzmatrix_col ltx_nopad_l ltx_nopad_r" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 25.7 0) translate(68.13,0) matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -65.36 -4.15)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:6.18em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="85.46"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="A5.F10.pic1.10" class="ltx_text">Group-Sum (<math id="A5.F10.pic1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=100</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></g></g></g></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span>Test Accuracy (eval mode) for MNIST with increasing number of classes. Results compare different output sizes <math id="A5.F10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>o</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o</annotation></semantics></math> and Group-Sum with varying <math id="A5.F10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.5 </span>Group-Sum Dropout</h3>

<div id="A5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many of our experimental models show overfitting tendencies. Even though the choice of <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> can help to partially mitigate it, there are other techniques that may be applied. One of them is dropout. Since we are mainly focused on the output layer, we apply dropout only to the Group-Sum layer. We do this by deactivating each neuron with a probability <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p\in\{0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.7</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p\in\{0.1,0.3,0.5,0.7,0.9\}</annotation></semantics></math> in each batch. Figure <a href="#A5.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ E.5 Group-Sum Dropout ‣ Appendix E Alternatives ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> shows the performance heatmap of various <math id="A5.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-dropout combinations on our synthetic dataset with 2000 different classes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We see that certain amounts of dropout increased performance considerably. For <math id="A5.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>, almost all test accuracies are superior to <math id="A5.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Not only that, but it seems to lessen the importance of an optimal <math id="A5.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, expanding high performance regions.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.F11" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.25933v1/synthetic_tau_dropout2.png" id="A5.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:329/235;" width="329" height="235" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 11: </span>Test performance heatmap of different <math id="A5.F11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>-dropout combinations. The performance is measured on our synthetic dataset with 2000 different classes.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.6 </span>Tree-based Prediction</h3>

<div id="A5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Instead of summing up each part of each class, we try to decrease the parts’ size by using class-specific DLGN. This halves the parts’ dimension with each layer for a certain number of layers before being summed up. We also use a class-specific DLGN that uses the whole output layer for each output. Unfortunately, neither variants improve the model’s performance. Additionally, using the whole output layer for each class quickly becomes computationally infeasible for many classes and a large output layer dimension.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Numeric Results</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section displays the resulting accuracies for many of our tested models, including the methods in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Alternatives ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>. We show accuracy for all MNIST datasets (<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Numeric Results ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendices</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Numeric Results ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Numeric Results ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Numeric Results ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Numeric Results ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> and <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Numeric Results ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>) as well as CIFAR10 (<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Numeric Results ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>) and CIFAR100 (<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Numeric Results ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>). All models were trained with the same input representation as explained in <a href="#S4" title="4 Methodology and Experimental Setup ‣ From MNIST to ImageNet: Understanding the Scalability Boundaries of Differentiable Logic Gate Networks" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. DLGNs can only make use of hardware-accelerated inference with binary input representation. To mitigate comparisons of models with inconsistent amounts of input information, we show both the accuracy with binary and continuous input representations. The discrete settings use binary inputs and discrete models for logic gate-based models. <span id="A6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fully Connected Last Layer</span> refers to a standard DLGN, with appended fully-connected layers during training. <span id="A6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Run</span> 1, 2, and 3 refer to appended layers with {512}, {512, 512}, and {1000, 100}, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_middle" style="width:194.8pt;">
      
